---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const shadesHref = toolPathBySlug('color-shades-generator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you copy an OKLCH CSS gradient? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Type two colors. The page blends them in OKLCH and puts the CSS on one line.
      Copy CSS is the declaration to paste. Blue #0000ff to yellow #ffff00 is the pair that opens the page.
    </p>
    <p>
      The strip is painted from sampled hex stops, so you can see the blend before you paste.
      Copying an OKLCH linear gradient into a stylesheet is the job the button is for.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Perceptual Gradient Generator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-oklch-is">
    <h2>What is an OKLCH CSS gradient? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An OKLCH CSS gradient is a linear gradient whose stops are blended in OKLCH.
      Lightness, chroma, and hue move from the start color to the end color.
      The copied line names that space, so a supporting browser does the same blend.
    </p>
    <p>
      The declaration reads linear-gradient, to the right, in oklch, then the two hex stops.
      Nine samples sit under the strip. Each sample is a hex you can copy on its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      It works by stepping lightness, chroma, and hue, then writing the declaration.
      For example, this css gradient generator oklch interpolates #0000ff to #ffff00.
      Input: blue #0000ff and yellow #ffff00 as the two stops.
    </p>
    <p>
      Output: one linear gradient generator line, plus sampled hex stops.
      Browsers that lack OKLCH interpolation still paint the strip from those samples.
      The sRGB midpoint is the gray the note compares with the OKLCH middle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gray-middle">
    <h2>Why does blue to yellow go gray? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Checking whether blue to yellow goes muddy in sRGB is why that pair is the default.
      Channel blending walks red, green, and blue in a straight line.
      Those channels do not track how bright a color looks, so the middle loses chroma.
    </p>
    <p>
      The note fires when the OKLCH middle keeps at least 0.04 more chroma than the sRGB middle.
      Two grays, such as #777777 to #bbbbbb, stay quiet because there was little chroma to lose.
      A clamped sample is named only when the middle is not muddy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="oklch-vs-srgb">
    <h2>What is the difference between an OKLCH gradient and an sRGB gradient? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The difference between an OKLCH gradient and an sRGB gradient is which numbers get blended.
      OKLCH blends lightness, chroma, and hue along the short way around the hue circle.
      sRGB blends the three channels, and that is the mix that goes gray between saturated stops.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sampling hex stops when a browser does not interpolate in OKLCH is what the row under the strip is for.
      Paste the OKLCH line when the browser understands it. Keep the samples when it does not.
      A shade scale from one stop is a different page: the <a href={shadesHref}>color shades generator</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes">
    <h2>Which gradient mistakes show up late? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="The gray middle, copied">
      <p>
        Copying an sRGB blend when the middle has gone gray ships a muddy bar that looked even in a preview.
        The note names the chroma drop so that blend is not the one you paste.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Samples treated as the declaration">
      <p>
        Assuming the hex samples are the declaration to paste swaps the fallback for the OKLCH line.
        Copy CSS is the line with "in oklch". The samples are the strip.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Stops sent to a server">
      <p>
        Uploading stops to a generator that stores them is a different product.
        These two colors stay in the page. Nothing is uploaded.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The copied line names the space">
      <p>
        Keep the words "in oklch" in the declaration you paste.
        Without them, the browser blends channels and the gray middle comes back.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="On your device">
      <p>
        Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded.
        The page does not apply a vendor easing curve.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
